'''''Euphorbia leucocephala''''', with many [[common name]]s including '''little Christmas flower''', '''white lace euphorbia''', '''snow bush''', '''snow flake''', '''snows of Kilimanjaro'''<ref>{{citation |url=http://zipcodezoo.com/index.php/Euphorbia_leucocephala |title=Zipcodezoo |access-date=23 December 2015}}</ref> and '''white Christmas bush'''<ref>{{citation |url=https://scholarspace.manoa.hawaii.edu/bitstream/handle/10125/37717/No_07_January1972.pdf?sequence=1 |title=Growth Regulator Notes |journal=Horticultural Digest, University of Hawaii |author=Richard A. Criley |volume=7 |year=1972}}</ref> is a species of [[plant]] in the family [[Euphorbiaceae]]. It is [[Endemism|endemic]] to [[Mexico]] and [[Mesoamerica]], and a relative of the [[poinsettia]].<ref>{{citation |url=https://npgsweb.ars-grin.gov/gringlobal/taxonomydetail.aspx?id=16384 |title=USDA GRIN Taxonomy |access-date=23 December 2015}}</ref> Its clear [[sap]] is an irritant that can cause [[blisters]] and skin [[Rash|rashes]]. Ingesting it can cause [[vomiting]] and [[diarrhea]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Snowflake (Euphorbia leucocephala) |url=https://www.childrens.health.qld.gov.au/poisonous-plant-snowflake-euphorbia-leucocephala/}}</ref>

